__d(function(e,a,r,o,t,i,n){t.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Social Science",textbook:"Social and Political Life \u2014 Part I (Civics)",chapter_number:28,chapter_title:"Rural Livelihoods",total_cards:20,cards:[{id:"fc01",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a livelihood? Give three examples of rural livelihoods.",back:"A livelihood is the means by which a person earns their income and meets their basic needs for food, shelter, and clothing. Three examples of rural livelihoods: (1) Farming \u2014 growing crops for self-consumption or sale. (2) Artisan crafts \u2014 weaving, pottery, carpentry. (3) Fishing \u2014 coastal or river fishing for sale or consumption. Also: dairy farming, livestock rearing, shopkeeping, seasonal migration.",memory_hook:"Livelihood = the way you live your day to earn your daily bread. Rural livelihoods = as many ways to earn as there are people in a village."},{id:"fc02",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Name the four types of farmers classified by their relationship to land.",back:"Four types: (1) Large Landowner / Big Farmer \u2014 owns significant land, hires labour, sells surplus. (2) Small/Marginal Farmer \u2014 owns tiny plots (under 2 hectares), farms for family consumption (subsistence). (3) Tenant Farmer \u2014 does not own land, rents it from a landowner, pays rent as crop share or cash. (4) Agricultural Labourer \u2014 owns no land, works for daily wages on others' fields \u2014 the most vulnerable.",memory_hook:"Farmer ladder from top to bottom: Large Landowner (owns much) \u2192 Small Farmer (owns little) \u2192 Tenant Farmer (rents) \u2192 Agricultural Labourer (nothing). Land ownership = position on the ladder."},{id:"fc03",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is subsistence farming? Why do small farmers practise it?",back:"Subsistence farming is farming primarily to produce food for the family's own consumption \u2014 with little or no surplus left to sell. Small farmers practise subsistence farming because their land is too small to produce a large surplus; if they sold everything, they would have nothing to eat. They farm to survive, not to make profit. It provides food security but no cash income \u2014 they must find other ways to earn money.",memory_hook:"Subsistence = survival farming. 'Sub' = under (barely enough). Subsistence farming = barely enough to eat, nothing extra to sell. It keeps the family alive, not wealthy."},{id:"fc04",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Who is an agricultural labourer? Why are they considered the most economically vulnerable group in rural India?",back:"An agricultural labourer owns no land and earns daily wages by working on others' fields, especially during peak seasons (sowing, weeding, harvesting). They are most vulnerable because: (1) No land = no food security or asset. (2) Seasonal work = no income during off-season. (3) Daily wages = no job security. (4) No collateral = cannot get bank loans; must borrow from moneylenders at high interest. (5) First to suffer in drought or crop failure.",memory_hook:"Agricultural labourer = the bottom rung. No land, no lease, no guarantee \u2014 just their hands and the hope that someone needs workers today."},{id:"fc05",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a tenant farmer and what are the risks they face?",back:"A tenant farmer does not own land but rents it from a landowner, paying rent as a share of the crop (sharecropping) or as cash. Risks: (1) Eviction \u2014 the landowner can take the land back at any time. (2) High rent \u2014 paying 50% of the crop as rent leaves very little. (3) No investment security \u2014 cannot invest in permanent improvements (irrigation, soil health) on land they do not own. (4) Cannot get formal bank loans (no land ownership as collateral).",memory_hook:"Tenant farmer = farming on borrowed ground. Like renting a house \u2014 you can be asked to leave at any time. And you cannot paint the walls (invest) without the landlord's permission."},{id:"fc06",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is MGNREGA and why is it important for rural livelihoods?",back:"MGNREGA (Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act) guarantees 100 days of wage employment per year to every rural household. It is important because: (1) Provides income during agricultural lean seasons when farm work is unavailable. (2) Gives agricultural labourers \u2014 who have no other income in off-season \u2014 a minimum income guarantee. (3) Creates rural assets (roads, ponds, trees, drainage) as the public works project. (4) Gives workers legal right to demand work \u2014 within 15 days of application or compensation is paid.",memory_hook:"MGNREGA = 100 days of guaranteed income. For agricultural labourers, it is the difference between eating and not eating during off-season. 100 days = 33% of the year covered."},{id:"fc07",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why do small farmers often fall into debt traps?",back:"Debt trap cycle: (1) Small farmer needs money for seeds, fertiliser, and pesticides before the season \u2014 borrows from a moneylender at 24%+ annual interest. (2) If the crop is good, she earns enough to repay \u2014 but barely. (3) If rains fail or prices crash, she cannot repay the loan. (4) Interest compounds \u2014 the next year she owes more. (5) She borrows again for the next season \u2014 the debt grows. (6) Eventually, she may have to sell her land or face permanent debt. This cycle drives rural poverty and is linked to farmer distress across India.",memory_hook:"Debt trap = a hole that gets deeper each year. Borrow to plant. Fail to repay. Borrow more. Fail again. The debt grows while the farmer shrinks."},{id:"fc08",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the Minimum Support Price (MSP) and how does it help farmers?",back:"MSP (Minimum Support Price) is the price floor set by the central government for specific agricultural crops. If market prices fall below MSP, the government (through agencies like FCI \u2014 Food Corporation of India, or state agencies like KFCSC in Karnataka) purchases the crop from farmers at this guaranteed minimum price. MSP prevents farmers from being forced to sell at very low prices after a bumper harvest. However, MSP is only announced for a limited number of crops and actual procurement is not universal.",memory_hook:"MSP = the safety net under the price tightrope. If market price falls, MSP catches the farmer. Without MSP, a bumper harvest can actually bankrupt a farmer \u2014 too much supply, prices crash."},{id:"fc09",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why does a good harvest sometimes lead to lower incomes for farmers?",back:"In economics, this is called the 'price paradox': when all farmers have a good harvest simultaneously, the total supply of the crop (e.g., tomatoes, onions) in the market increases dramatically. More supply with same demand = lower prices. The farmer sold 100 kg at \u20b920/kg in a bad year (income \u20b92,000). In a good year they sell 200 kg at \u20b95/kg (income \u20b91,000). They worked harder and grew more but earned LESS. This is why price stabilisation through MSP and storage infrastructure are critical for farmer welfare.",memory_hook:"Good harvest + no storage + falling prices = less income. Farmer's cruel paradox: the better the crop, the worse the price. That is why MSP and cold storage are not luxuries \u2014 they are survival tools."},{id:"fc10",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Name four Karnataka-specific rural livelihoods connected to this chapter.",back:"(1) Sericulture \u2014 rearing silkworms for silk production in Ramanagara, Kolar, Mysuru, and Tumakuru districts. (2) Coffee cultivation \u2014 in the malnad (hilly) areas of Kodagu, Hassan, and Shivamogga. (3) Coastal fishing \u2014 traditional and mechanised sea fishing in Dakshina Kannada, Udupi, and Uttara Kannada. (4) Artisan crafts \u2014 Channapatna toy making (Ramanagara), Bidriware (Bidar), Ilkal handloom sarees (Bagalkot), Kasuti embroidery (Dharwad/Gadag).",memory_hook:"Karnataka's rural livelihood rainbow: Silk (south), Coffee (malnad), Fish (coast), Crafts (across the state). Each region has its own livelihood signature."},{id:"fc11",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What challenges do rural artisans face in maintaining their livelihoods?",back:"Rural artisans \u2014 potters, weavers, blacksmiths, carpenters \u2014 face: (1) Competition from cheap factory-made goods \u2014 plastic pots replace clay pots; power-loom sarees undercut handloom; machine-made furniture undercuts carpenters. (2) Rising input costs \u2014 raw materials (thread, clay, metals) become more expensive while selling prices stagnate. (3) Market access \u2014 artisans in remote villages cannot reach city consumers; middlemen capture profits. (4) Lack of skilled successors \u2014 younger generations prefer other jobs. (5) No credit access \u2014 cannot borrow to buy better tools or materials.",memory_hook:"Artisan threats = CRMS: Competition (factory goods), Rising costs, Market access, Successors (skilled youth leaving). GI tags and government craft fairs help counter these threats."},{id:"fc12",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How does Karnataka's KMF-Nandini cooperative benefit dairy farmers?",back:"KMF (Karnataka Cooperative Milk Producers' Federation) \u2014 branded Nandini \u2014 is a dairy cooperative that: procures milk from over 25 lakh farmer members across Karnataka at fair prices set daily; provides farmers a reliable, daily income (unlike seasonal crop income); offers veterinary services, cattle feed, and artificial insemination to improve milk yield; provides insurance for cattle; and processes and markets milk products (Nandini milk, curd, butter, ghee, ice cream) across Karnataka and beyond. For small farmers, selling milk daily to KMF provides regular cash income even during agricultural off-season.",memory_hook:"KMF-Nandini = the farmer's daily ATM. Every morning, the milk is collected and every month, the payment arrives \u2014 steady income regardless of monsoon."},{id:"fc13",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why do many rural poor migrate seasonally to cities?",back:"Seasonal migration happens because: (1) Agricultural work is seasonal \u2014 3\u20134 months of active farming, then off-season with no farm income. (2) Agricultural labourers have no income during off-season \u2014 even MGNREGA's 100 days is not enough. (3) Cities offer construction work, domestic work, and small trade \u2014 income that is available year-round. (4) Rural wages are lower than urban wages for similar physical work. Seasonal migrants leave their village in November\u2013May (after harvest, before monsoon) and return for sowing and harvest seasons. This migration is a coping strategy for rural poverty.",memory_hook:"Seasonal migration = following the money. Farm work ends \u2192 city work begins \u2192 rains come \u2192 return for sowing. The rural poor are not tied to one place \u2014 they follow livelihoods."},{id:"fc14",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"A family in Karnataka's Bidar district practises Bidriware craft. What challenges might they face and what support can help?",back:"Challenges: competition from cheap metal souvenirs made in factories; rising cost of zinc, copper, and silver; difficulty reaching urban consumers; younger family members preferring other careers. Support available: (1) Bidriware has a GI (Geographical Indication) tag \u2014 legally, only Bidar craftspeople can sell products as 'Bidriware,' protecting against imitation. (2) Karnataka government's Kalamantapa runs craft fairs (Shilpotsav) giving artisans market access. (3) Rozgar Yojane and PM Vishwakarma scheme give artisans access to credit. (4) Online platforms like Amazon's 'Karigar' and government's GeM portal list traditional crafts.",memory_hook:"Bidriware = unique Karnataka metalwork. GI tag = its legal shield. Craft fairs + online platforms = its market bridge. Without these supports, even the most beautiful craft can die."},{id:"fc15",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Compare the income security of a large landowner and an agricultural labourer during a drought year.",back:"Large landowner during drought: has savings from previous surplus years; may have access to irrigation (bore well, canal) that reduces drought impact; can get formal bank loans at low interest; can diversify into livestock; has political connections to access relief quickly. Agricultural labourer during drought: no savings; no irrigation access; no bank loan (no collateral); MGNREGA work may be the only income \u2014 and demand for MGNREGA rises sharply during drought; risk of hunger is high. Drought hits everyone \u2014 but it destroys the agricultural labourer's livelihood while only denting the large landowner's income.",memory_hook:"Drought is a filter: it reveals who has buffers (large landowners) and who has none (agricultural labourers). The same rain failure = minor setback for rich, catastrophe for poor."},{id:"fc16",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How do middlemen affect farmers' income?",back:"Middlemen are traders or commission agents who buy crops from farmers and sell to retailers or consumers in cities. Problem: they buy low and sell high. Example \u2014 a farmer sells tomatoes at \u20b95/kg (farmgate price). The middleman transports them to the city and sells to a market vendor for \u20b925/kg. The vendor sells to consumers at \u20b940/kg. The farmer gets only \u20b95 of the \u20b940 the consumer pays \u2014 the remaining \u20b935 is shared by middlemen and vendors. To reduce middleman dependence: farmer producer organisations (FPOs), direct sale at mandis, e-NAM (electronic national agriculture market) platform.",memory_hook:"Middleman math: farmer gets \u20b95, consumer pays \u20b940. The gap (\u20b935) = middleman profit. Every rupee the middleman earns is a rupee the farmer did not get."},{id:"fc17",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why is coffee an important rural livelihood in Karnataka's malnad region?",back:"Karnataka is India's largest coffee-growing state, producing about 70% of national coffee output. Coffee is grown primarily in the malnad (hilly, forested) region \u2014 Kodagu (Coorg), Hassan, Chikkamagaluru, and Shivamogga districts. Coffee provides: regular income from biennial harvests (arabica in November, robusta October\u2013January); employment for lakhs of seasonal coffee-plucking workers (many from tribal communities); foreign exchange through exports. However, coffee is vulnerable to price volatility in global markets (coffee is traded internationally) and climate change (malnad rainfall patterns are shifting).",memory_hook:"Karnataka = India's coffee capital. 70% of India's coffee grows in Karnataka's malnad hills \u2014 the same hills where Karnataka's forest communities have lived for centuries."},{id:"fc18",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How does a GI (Geographical Indication) tag protect rural artisans' livelihoods?",back:"A GI tag is a legal certification that a product with specific qualities or reputation originates from a particular geographical area. For rural artisans, it means: (1) Only artisans from that area can legally sell the product under that name \u2014 e.g., only Bidar craftspeople can sell 'Bidriware'; only Mysuru weavers can sell 'Mysore Silk Saree.' (2) It prevents cheap imitations from being sold under the authentic name. (3) It allows premium pricing \u2014 GI-tagged products can command higher prices in markets. (4) It builds brand recognition nationally and internationally. Karnataka has GI tags for: Mysore Silk, Bidriware, Channapatna Toys, Ilkal Sarees, Coorg Orange, Navalgund Durries.",memory_hook:"GI tag = artisan's legal fortress. Without it, anyone can copy and sell fake 'Mysore Silk' undercutting the real weaver. With it, the authentic product is legally protected."},{id:"fc19",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"A village has 200 farming families. 50 own large farms, 80 own small plots, 40 rent land, and 30 are agricultural labourers. Which group is most at risk in a drought year and what government scheme would help them most?",back:"The 30 agricultural labourers are most at risk \u2014 they have no land, no savings, and their wages disappear when farm work stops during drought. The 40 tenant farmers are next \u2014 rents may still be demanded even if crops fail. MGNREGA would help most: it guarantees 100 days of wage employment even when agricultural work is absent. During drought years, MGNREGA work on water conservation projects (ponds, check dams, bund repair) provides income AND creates assets that reduce future drought impact. In Karnataka, demand for MGNREGA spikes sharply in Raichur, Vijayapura, Yadgir, and Bidar during drought years.",memory_hook:"Drought + agricultural labourers = maximum crisis. MGNREGA = government's drought income lifeline. The scheme becomes most important exactly when the rain does not come."},{id:"fc20",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why should urban students care about the livelihoods of rural farmers and artisans?",back:"Urban residents should care because: (1) All food comes from rural farmers \u2014 rice, dal, vegetables, milk, coffee. Without secure farmer livelihoods, urban food security is at risk. (2) Traditional crafts (Channapatna toys, Bidriware, Kasuti embroidery) are irreplaceable cultural heritage \u2014 if artisans cannot earn, these crafts will disappear. (3) India's development is incomplete if 60% of its workforce earns insecure incomes. (4) Informed urban citizens can make choices: buy handloom over power-loom, reduce food waste, support fair trade. (5) Future policy-makers, doctors, engineers, and administrators from cities must understand rural India to serve it well.",memory_hook:"Urban life runs on rural work. Your rice, your coffee, your silk saree \u2014 all rural. Caring about rural livelihoods = caring about the hands that feed and clothe you."}]}},2937,[]);